## Notes / gotchas

- **Header injection:** a token containing `\r\n` or `"` could split or corrupt the header. The regex above rejects those characters — don't skip validation.
- **Don't log the token.** Redact it in logs: `header.replace(/Bearer .+/, "Bearer ***")`.
- **HTTPS only.** Bearer tokens are sent in cleartext over plain HTTP.
- **Browsers:** you can't set `Authorization` manually in `fetch` from a page — it's a forbidden header name. Use `credentials: "include"` with cookies, or proxy through your backend.
- **Avoid putting tokens in query strings** (`?access_token=...`); they end up in server logs, browser history, and `Referer` headers.
- If your token might contain characters outside `b64token` (some non-standard providers do), base64url-encode it first rather than loosening the check.

```js
/**
 * Builds an Authorization header value for Bearer token auth.
 * @param {string} token - The raw access token (without the "Bearer " prefix).
 * @returns {string} e.g. "Bearer eyJhbGciOi..."
 * @throws {TypeError} if the token is not a string.
 * @throws {Error} if the token is empty or contains invalid characters.
 */
function buildBearerAuthHeader(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string') {
    throw new TypeError('Token must be a string');
  }

  const trimmed = token.trim();
  if (trimmed.length === 0) {
    throw new Error('Token must not be empty');
  }

  // Whitespace (including CR/LF) would corrupt the header or allow injection.
  if (/\s/.test(trimmed)) {
    throw new Error('Token must not contain whitespace');
  }

  return `Bearer ${trimmed}`;
}
```

### Usage

```js
const token = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...';

const headers = {
  Authorization: buildBearerAuthHeader(token),
  'Content-Type': 'application/json',
};

await fetch('/api/profile', { headers });
```

### Notes

- **Don't double-prefix.** If your input might already include `Bearer `, strip it first:
  ```js
  const raw = token.replace(/^Bearer\s+/i, '');
  const header = buildBearerAuthHeader(raw);
  ```
- **Header injection guard.** The whitespace check rejects tokens containing spaces, tabs, `\r`, or `\n` — these would either break the header or let an attacker inject extra headers/requests.
- **Don't log the result.** The returned string contains a live credential; avoid writing it to logs or error messages.
- **Server-side.** In Node.js, set it via `res.setHeader('Authorization', value)`; in Express, `res.set('Authorization', value)`.

## The function

```js
/**
 * Builds an HTTP `Authorization` header value for Bearer token auth.
 *
 * @param {string} token - The raw access token (without the "Bearer " prefix).
 * @returns {string} The header value, e.g. "Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..."
 * @throws {TypeError}  If `token` is not a string.
 * @throws {RangeError} If `token` is empty or contains characters that are
 *                     illegal in a bearer token (e.g. CR/LF, spaces).
 */
export function buildBearerAuthHeader(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string') {
    throw new TypeError('token must be a string');
  }

  const trimmed = token.trim();

  if (trimmed.length === 0) {
    throw new RangeError('token must not be empty');
  }

  // RFC 6750 b64token: 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "." / "_" / "~" / "+" / "/" ) *"="
  // Anything else (spaces, CR/LF, quotes, commas) would be invalid or could
  // allow header injection.
  if (!/^[A-Za-z0-9\-._~+/]+=*$/.test(trimmed)) {
    throw new RangeError('token contains characters that are not valid in a bearer token');
  }

  return `Bearer ${trimmed}`;
}
```

## Usage

```js
const authHeader = buildBearerAuthHeader(accessToken);

const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/v1/me', {
  headers: {
    Authorization: authHeader,
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
});
```

If you want a ready-made headers object instead of just the value:

```js
export function bearerHeaders(token, extra = {}) {
  return { ...extra, Authorization: buildBearerAuthHeader(token) };
}
```

## Notes

- **Don't include the `Bearer ` prefix in the input.** The function adds it, so passing `"Bearer abc"` would produce `"Bearer Bearer abc"`. If your source may or may not include the prefix, strip it first:

  ```js
  const raw = token.replace(/^Bearer\s+/i, '');
  ```

- **JWTs are covered by the same regex** — the `.` and `-`/`_` characters in the base64url payload are all in the allowed set.

- **Validation is a safety net, not a security check.** It prevents malformed headers and CRLF/header-injection issues, but it does not verify the token's signature or expiry — that's the server's job.

- **Never log the returned value.** If you need to debug, log a truncated fingerprint instead:

  ```js
  console.debug(`auth header for ${token.slice(0, 4)}…`);
  ```

```js
/**
 * Builds an HTTP Authorization header value for Bearer token auth.
 *
 * @param {string} token - The bearer token (e.g., an OAuth2 access token).
 * @returns {string} The header value, e.g. "Bearer abc123".
 * @throws {TypeError} If the token is not a string.
 * @throws {Error} If the token is empty or contains illegal characters.
 */
function buildBearerAuthHeader(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string') {
    throw new TypeError('Token must be a string');
  }

  const value = token.trim();

  if (value.length === 0) {
    throw new Error('Token must not be empty');
  }

  // Guard against header/CRLF injection.
  if (/[\r\n]/.test(value)) {
    throw new Error('Token must not contain CR or LF characters');
  }

  return `Bearer ${value}`;
}
```

**Usage:**

```js
const headerValue = buildBearerAuthHeader('ya29.a0AfH6SMBx...');

fetch('https://api.example.com/resource', {
  headers: {
    Authorization: headerValue,
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
});
// Authorization: Bearer ya29.a0AfH6SMBx...
```

**Optional stricter variant** — if you know the token is an RFC 6750 `b64token`, you can validate the character set:

```js
const B64TOKEN = /^[A-Za-z0-9\-._~+/]+=*$/;

function buildBearerAuthHeader(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string' || !B64TOKEN.test(token.trim())) {
    throw new Error('Invalid bearer token');
  }
  return `Bearer ${token.trim()}`;
}
```

Notes:
- The scheme name `Bearer` is case-insensitive per RFC 7235, but `Bearer` is the conventional casing.
- Don't put the token in a query string; use the `Authorization` header (or a cookie) to avoid leaking it in logs and referrers.
- If the token comes from user input or an untrusted source, validate/sanitize it before use, and never log the resulting header value.

Here's a straightforward implementation, with a bit of input validation and a convenience wrapper for `fetch`:

```js
/**
 * Builds an Authorization header value for Bearer token authentication.
 *
 * @param {string} token - The access token (without the "Bearer " prefix).
 * @returns {string} The header value, e.g. "Bearer eyJhbGciOi..."
 * @throws {TypeError} If the token is missing or not a string.
 * @throws {Error} If the token is empty or contains whitespace/control chars.
 */
function buildBearerAuthHeader(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string') {
    throw new TypeError('token must be a string');
  }

  // Trim surrounding whitespace, but reject tokens with internal whitespace,
  // since a raw header value can't contain spaces unescaped.
  const trimmed = token.trim();

  if (trimmed === '') {
    throw new Error('token must not be empty');
  }
  if (/\s/.test(trimmed)) {
    throw new Error('token must not contain whitespace');
  }
  // eslint-disable-next-line no-control-regex
  if (/[\x00-\x1F\x7F]/.test(trimmed)) {
    throw new Error('token must not contain control characters');
  }

  return `Bearer ${trimmed}`;
}

/**
 * Convenience: returns a headers object ready to spread into a fetch() call.
 */
function bearerHeaders(token) {
  return {
    Authorization: buildBearerAuthHeader(token),
  };
}
```

Usage:

```js
const token = 'eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...';

fetch('https://api.example.com/me', {
  headers: bearerHeaders(token),
})
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .then(console.log);
```

Notes:

- **Don't log the result.** The returned string contains your credential; avoid putting it in logs, error messages, or telemetry.
- **Use HTTPS.** A bearer token sent over plain HTTP is sent in the clear.
- **Prefer `fetch` over `XMLHttpRequest`** for modern code, and let `fetch` handle the header encoding.
- If your token can contain characters that aren't valid in a header value (rare for JWTs, but possible for opaque tokens), base64url-encode it first or pass it via a `Headers` object, which validates for you.

If you want the function to also accept an already-prefixed value like `"Bearer abc123"`, you can add a check at the top:

```js
if (/^Bearer\s+/i.test(trimmed)) {
  throw new Error('token should not include the "Bearer " prefix');
}
```

```js
/**
 * Builds an Authorization header value for Bearer token authentication.
 *
 * @param {string} token - The access token.
 * @returns {string} The header value, e.g. "Bearer abc123".
 * @throws {TypeError} If the token is not a non-empty string.
 */
function buildBearerAuthorization(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string' || token.trim() === '') {
    throw new TypeError('Token must be a non-empty string');
  }

  const value = token.trim();

  // Reject characters that could enable header injection (CR/LF) or break the format.
  if (/[\r\n]/.test(value)) {
    throw new TypeError('Token must not contain CR or LF characters');
  }

  return `Bearer ${value}`;
}
```

**Usage:**

```js
const authHeader = buildBearerAuthorization('abc123');
// => "Bearer abc123"

fetch('/api/protected', {
  headers: {
    Authorization: buildBearerAuthorization(token),
  },
});
```

Notes:
- The token is trimmed of surrounding whitespace, since leading/trailing spaces are not valid in a Bearer credential.
- CR/LF characters are rejected to prevent header injection (HTTP response splitting) if the token ever comes from untrusted input.
- The `Bearer` scheme is case-insensitive per RFC 6750, but `Bearer` is the conventional casing.

## Basic implementation

```js
/**
 * Builds an HTTP `Authorization` header value for Bearer token auth.
 * @param {string} token - The raw access token (e.g. a JWT or opaque token).
 * @returns {string} e.g. "Bearer eyJhbGciOi..."
 * @throws {TypeError} if token isn't a string
 * @throws {Error} if token is empty or contains illegal header characters
 */
function buildBearerAuthHeader(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string') {
    throw new TypeError('Bearer token must be a string');
  }

  const value = token.trim();

  if (value.length === 0) {
    throw new Error('Bearer token must not be empty');
  }

  // Prevent header injection (CRLF) and other control characters.
  // eslint-disable-next-line no-control-regex
  if (/[\u0000-\u001F\u007F\r\n]/.test(value)) {
    throw new Error('Bearer token contains invalid control characters');
  }

  return `Bearer ${value}`;
}
```

### Usage

```js
const header = buildBearerAuthHeader('eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.e30.xYz');

await fetch('https://api.example.com/me', {
  headers: { Authorization: header },
});
```

## Stricter variant (validates RFC 6750 `b64token` syntax)

Some APIs require the token to match the `token68` grammar: `ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "." / "_" / "~" / "+" / "/"`, optionally padded with `=`.

```js
const B64TOKEN_RE = /^[A-Za-z0-9\-._~+/]+=*$/;

function buildBearerAuthHeader(token, { strict = false } = {}) {
  const value = buildBearerAuthHeader(token); // reuse base validation

  if (strict && !B64TOKEN_RE.test(value.slice('Bearer '.length))) {
    throw new Error('Token is not a valid RFC 6750 b64token');
  }

  return value;
}
```

## Notes

- **Don't** use `btoa()` on arbitrary tokens — it fails on non-Latin1 characters. If you need to encode a token yourself, use `Buffer.from(token, 'utf8').toString('base64')` (Node) or `btoa(String.fromCharCode(...new TextEncoder().encode(token)))` (browser).
- **Never** log the returned header value; it contains the secret.
- If you need to send a token in a query string or cookie instead, that's a different mechanism — this function only covers the `Authorization` header.
- For Basic auth, the equivalent is `Basic ${Buffer.from(`${user}:${pass}`).toString('base64')}` — prefer Bearer whenever the server supports it.

## Implementation

```js
// RFC 6750 §2.1 — b64token grammar
const B64TOKEN_RE = /^[A-Za-z0-9\-._~+/]+=*$/;

/**
 * Builds an Authorization header value for Bearer token auth.
 *
 * @param {string} token - The raw access token (with or without a "Bearer " prefix)
 * @returns {string} e.g. "Bearer eyJhbGciOi..."
 * @throws {TypeError} if token is not a string
 * @throws {Error} if token is empty or contains characters illegal in a header value
 */
function buildBearerAuthorization(token) {
  if (typeof token !== 'string') {
    throw new TypeError('Bearer token must be a string');
  }

  // Trim surrounding whitespace, and tolerate a caller-supplied scheme prefix.
  const bare = token.trim().replace(/^Bearer\s+/i, '');

  if (bare.length === 0) {
    throw new Error('Bearer token must not be empty');
  }

  // Reject CR/LF, spaces, and other chars that could enable header injection
  // or produce a malformed header value.
  if (!B64TOKEN_RE.test(bare)) {
    throw new Error('Invalid bearer token: contains characters not allowed by RFC 6750');
  }

  return `Bearer ${bare}`;
}
```

## Usage

```js
buildBearerAuthorization('eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxIn0.abc-_~+/==');
// => "Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxIn0.abc-_~+/=="

buildBearerAuthorization('Bearer abc123');   // => "Bearer abc123"  (prefix tolerated)
buildBearerAuthorization('  abc123  ');      // => "Bearer abc123"  (trimmed)

buildBearerAuthorization('');        // throws Error
buildBearerAuthorization('abc 123');  // throws Error (embedded space)
buildBearerAuthorization('abc\r\nX-Injected: 1'); // throws Error (header injection)
buildBearerAuthorization(null);       // throws TypeError
```

## Fetch example

```js
const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/me', {
  headers: { Authorization: buildBearerAuthorization(await getAccessToken()) },
});
```

## Notes

- **Don't log the result.** The returned string is a credential; redact it in debug output and error messages.
- **The regex is a format check, not a security check.** It only guarantees the value is a syntactically valid `b64token` and can't break out of the header. Validate the token's authenticity (signature, issuer, audience, expiry) server-side.
- **Tokens are usually base64url** (JWTs use `-` and `_`), which the character class covers. If your provider issues a token with other characters, widen the regex deliberately rather than dropping the check.
- **In Node's `http`/`https` modules** the same value works as `headers: { Authorization: buildBearerAuthorization(token) }`.
